body:
I have tried the snapshot version from the repository you linked to, your commit is included there, but it does not quite work as expected.

The memory issue is solved, but now, something seems to block the creation of new test cases, even if there are worker threads available to run the actual test. I have a configuration with 16 worker threads and when I start my real test case, I seem to observe the following behaviour:

1. A number of test cases, less than 16, usual values are in the range 6-10, are pulled from the argument source.
2. These test are started concurrently.
3. Something now waits until all running tests are finished.
4. Repeat from 1.

I have added some logging to the original test case to try to show the behaviour:

```
@TestInstance(TestInstance.Lifecycle.PER_CLASS)
@Execution(CONCURRENT)
public class OOMTest {

    private static final AtomicInteger activeThreadCount = new AtomicInteger();
    private static final Random random = new Random();

    @ParameterizedTest
    @MethodSource
    public void test(int a, int b, int c, int d) {
        System.out.println("> number of tests currently running: " + activeThreadCount.incrementAndGet());
        try {
            Thread.sleep(2000 + random.nextInt(2000));
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
        System.out.println("< number of tests currently running: " + activeThreadCount.decrementAndGet());
    }

    public Stream<? extends Arguments> test() {
        return IntStream.range(0, 1000).boxed()
                .flatMap(a -> IntStream.range(0, 1000).boxed()
                        .flatMap(b -> IntStream.range(0, 1000).boxed()
                                .flatMap(c -> IntStream.range(0, 1000).boxed()
                                        .map(d -> {
                                            System.out.println("creating test for " + a + " " + b + " " + c + " " + d);
                                            return Arguments.of(a, b, c, d);
                                        }))));
    }

}

```

This test case has more or less the same runtime behaviour as our real test:

- Determining the test data in the argument source is relatively fast and cheap.
- The actual test case runs a random time between 2 and 4 seconds.

I have attached the log output from a run of this test case here:

[junit-concurrency.log](https://github.com/junit-team/junit5/files/2187820/junit-concurrency.log)

My initial observations were not quite correct, but at least at the start, you can see the following behaviour:

- Even with 16 threads configured, only 7 test cases are pulled from the argument source.
- These 7 tests are started, and the test runner now blocks, until all 7 test cases are finished.
- Not until now, further test cases are pulled from the argument source and started.
- The following runtime behaviour is a little bit chaotic, but as you can see from the log output, all 16 test runners are never used simultaneously. At most, 12 tests are running concurrently.
